\begin{frame}{Where we were: logistic regression as a single neuron (Section 5.8.1, Figure 5.4)}
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.62\textwidth]{5-neuron}\par
\vspace{-1pt}
\raggedright\small
Eq.~(5.35): $z=\theta_0+\sum_j\theta_jx_j$, $\hat p=\sigma(z)$; the loss
$\ln(1+e^{z})-yz$ of Eq.~(5.14) is one more node.
\begin{columns}[T]
\begin{column}{0.5\textwidth}
\begin{block}{The vocabulary}
\footnotesize
$x_j$ \emph{inputs}; $\theta_j$ \emph{weights}; $\theta_0$ \emph{bias} (the
weight of an input fixed at one); $z$ \emph{pre-activation}; $\sigma$
\emph{activation function}.  With the step function this is Rosenblatt's
perceptron; with the identity it is linear regression.
\end{block}
\end{column}
\begin{column}{0.48\textwidth}
\begin{block}{The learning rule, Eqs.~(5.36)--(5.37)}
\footnotesize
$\partial C_i/\partial\theta_j=(\hat p_i-y_i)\,x_{ij}$, so
$\theta_j\leftarrow\theta_j-\gamma(\hat p_i-y_i)x_{ij}$: \emph{error times the
input on the edge}.  The delta rule, SGD with $M=1$ on the cross entropy;
summed over $i$ it is $\bm{X}^{T}(\bm{p}-\bm{y})$, Eq.~(5.17).
\end{block}
\end{column}
\end{columns}
\end{frame}

\begin{frame}{The same picture, read backwards: the computational graph (Section 5.8.2)}
\small
\begin{columns}
\begin{column}{0.5\textwidth}
\includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{5-logreggraph}
\footnotesize Book Figure 5.5: the neuron redrawn as the evaluation trace of
Section 4.14, with the parameters as \emph{inputs}, the data $x,y$ as
constants on the edges, and the loss as output.  Trace, Eq.~(5.38):
$v_{-1}=\theta_0$, $v_0=\theta_1$, $v_1=v_0x$, $v_2=v_1+v_{-1}=z$,
$v_3=e^{v_2}$, $v_4=1+v_3$, $v_5=\ln v_4$, $v_6=yv_2$, $v_7=v_5-v_6=C_i$.
\end{column}
\begin{column}{0.48\textwidth}
\begin{block}{Two readings of one diagram}
\footnotesize
As a \textbf{model}: data flow left to right, parameters are fixed numbers
on the edges, a probability comes out.  As a \textbf{computational graph}:
same nodes, same edges, roles exchanged -- we differentiate with respect to
the parameters, the data are constants.  The one structural feature: $z=v_2$
fans out into the softplus branch and the label branch.
\end{block}
\begin{block}{Local derivatives, Eq.~(5.39) -- eight numbers}
\footnotesize
$\partial v_1/\partial v_0=x$, $\partial v_2/\partial v_1=\partial v_2/\partial v_{-1}=1$,
$\partial v_3/\partial v_2=v_3$, $\partial v_4/\partial v_3=1$,
$\partial v_5/\partial v_4=1/v_4$, $\partial v_6/\partial v_2=y$,
$\partial v_7/\partial v_5=1$, $\partial v_7/\partial v_6=-1$.  Everything
that follows combines these.
\end{block}
\end{column}
\end{columns}
\end{frame}

\begin{frame}{Forward sweep, reverse sweep -- with numbers (Eqs.~5.40--5.41)}
\small
Take $\bm{\theta}=(0.5,0.25)$, $x=2$, $y=1$: the tape holds $z=1$,
$v_3=2.718$, $v_4=3.718$, $v_5=1.313$, $C_i=0.313$, $p=v_3/v_4=0.731$.
\begin{columns}
\begin{column}{0.5\textwidth}
\begin{block}{Forward mode: seed $(\dot v_{-1},\dot v_0)=(1,0)$, Eq.~(5.40)}
\footnotesize
$\dot v_1=x\dot v_0=0$, $\dot v_2=1$, $\dot v_3=v_3$, $\dot v_4=v_3$,
$\dot v_5=v_3/v_4=p$, $\dot v_6=y$, $\dot v_7=p-y=-0.269$.\\
That is $\partial C_i/\partial\theta_0$ -- and only that.  Seed $(0,1)$ and
sweep again: every tangent is the one above times $x$, ending in
$x(p-y)=-0.538$.  \textbf{Two parameters, two sweeps}; $p$ parameters,
$p$ sweeps.
\end{block}
\end{column}
\begin{column}{0.48\textwidth}
\begin{block}{Reverse mode: $\bar v_7=1$, back along the tape, Eq.~(5.41)}
\footnotesize
$\bar v_5=1$, $\bar v_6=-1$, $\bar v_4=1/v_4$, $\bar v_3=1/v_4$,\\
$\bar v_2=\bar v_3v_3+\bar v_6y=v_3/v_4-y=p-y$ \quad(the fan-out sums),\\
$\bar v_1=\bar v_2$, $\bar v_{-1}=\bar v_2=p-y=-0.269$,
$\bar v_0=\bar v_1x=x(p-y)=-0.538$.\\
\textbf{One sweep, both derivatives}, read off at the input nodes.
\end{block}
\end{column}
\end{columns}
\vspace{2pt}
\begin{block}{What the reverse sweep has produced}
\footnotesize
At $v_2$ the two branches contribute $p$ and $-y$: the \emph{residual}
$\hat p-y$, which Section 5.4 obtained by differentiating the log-odds by
hand, appears as the sensitivity of the cost to the linear predictor,
produced mechanically by the fan-out rule.  From $v_2$ down to the parameters
the sweep only multiplies by the input on each edge, $1$ and $x$ -- the delta
rule, done by the machine.  Keep the name: \textbf{the adjoint of the
pre-activation}, $\bar z$.  Today it will be called $\delta$.
\end{block}
\end{frame}

\begin{frame}{The whole data set, and why every framework runs reverse mode (Eq.~5.42)}
\small
\begin{columns}
\begin{column}{0.5\textwidth}
The cost is $\sum_iC_i$: $n$ copies of the graph hanging from the
\emph{same} $p$ parameter nodes, feeding one summation node.
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Reverse:} the sum passes $\bar C_i=1$ to every copy, each
        produces its residual $r_i=p_i-y_i$ at $z_i$, each parameter node
        accumulates $\sum_iX_{ij}r_i$:
        \[
          \bar{\bm{\theta}}=\bm{X}^{T}(\bm{p}-\bm{y})
        \]
        in \emph{one} pass, $\mathcal{O}(np)$ -- one $\bm{X}\bm{\theta}$ forward,
        one $\bm{X}^{T}\bm{r}$ back.  Tape: the $n$ numbers $z_i$.
  \item \textbf{Forward:} seed $\bm{e}_j$, get one component $\sum_iX_{ij}r_i$;
        $p$ sweeps, $\mathcal{O}(np^{2})$.
\end{itemize}
\end{column}
\begin{column}{0.48\textwidth}
\begin{block}{Measured (book Section 5.8.2, JAX, $n=5000$)}
\footnotesize
\begin{tabular}{@{}lrrr@{}}
\toprule
$p$ & value & reverse & forward\\
\midrule
$20$   & $0.13$ ms & $0.19$ ms & $0.41$ ms\\
$200$  & $0.32$ ms & $0.47$ ms & $4.53$ ms\\
$1000$ & $1.59$ ms & $3.31$ ms & $85.9$ ms\\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}\\[3pt]
Reverse: $1.4$--$2$ evaluations of $C$ whatever $p$ -- the cheap gradient
principle, Prop.~4.2.  Forward: $3$, $14$, $54$ evaluations, growing with
$p$.  Both agree with $\bm{X}^{T}(\bm{p}-\bm{y})$ to $10^{-16}$.
\end{block}
\begin{block}{The rule}
\footnotesize
Forward mode: one input direction to all outputs.  Reverse mode: one output
to all inputs.  A cost has one output and $p$ inputs -- training asks the
reverse-mode question.  Price: the tape.  A network with $10^{6}$ weights
asks it $10^{6}$ times more loudly.
\end{block}
\end{column}
\end{columns}
\end{frame}

\begin{frame}[fragile]{Code: the trace, both sweeps, and JAX (Section 5.8.2; \texttt{week40.ipynb})}
\begin{lstlisting}
def cross_entropy_one(theta0, theta1, x, y):     # the trace of Eq. (5.38), one node per line
    v1 = theta1 * x;  v2 = v1 + theta0;  v3 = jnp.exp(v2);  v4 = 1.0 + v3
    v5 = jnp.log(v4);  v6 = y * v2
    return v5 - v6

def reverse_sweep(theta0, theta1, x, y):         # Eq. (5.41) by hand
    v1 = theta1 * x; v2 = v1 + theta0; v3 = np.exp(v2); v4 = 1.0 + v3   # forward, onto the tape
    b7 = 1.0; b5, b6 = b7, -b7; b4 = b5 / v4; b3 = b4
    b2 = b3 * v3 + b6 * y                        # fan-out: two successors, one sum
    return b2, b2 * x                            # dC/dtheta0, dC/dtheta1

cost_one = lambda th: cross_entropy_one(th[0], th[1], 2.0, 1.0)
theta = jnp.array([0.5, 0.25])
for k in range(2):                               # forward mode: one sweep per parameter
    _, tangent = jax.jvp(cost_one, (theta,), (jnp.eye(2)[k],))
print(jax.grad(cost_one)(theta))                 # reverse mode: one sweep, both -> [-0.269 -0.538]

def cost(theta, X, y):                           # the whole data set, Eq. (5.43)
    z = X @ theta
    return jnp.mean(jnp.logaddexp(0.0, z) - y * z)
grad_ad = jax.grad(cost)(theta_p, X, y)          # == X.T @ (sigmoid(X @ theta_p) - y) / n
\end{lstlisting}
\footnotesize
\texttt{jax.grad(cost)} \emph{is} the gradient of Eq.~(5.17).  Hold on to the
pattern: we write the forward pass, the library writes the backward one.  In
fifty minutes we will have written the backward pass ourselves for a network
-- and checked it against this.
\end{frame}

\begin{insight}[one layer more]
\question{Suppose the inputs $x_j$ of the neuron in Figure 5.4 were not the
raw features but the outputs of another layer of such neurons,
$x_j=\sigma(\bm{w}_j^{T}\bm{u}+b_j)$.  What does the reverse sweep do when it
reaches $\bar z=\hat p-y$ -- and what is the name of the algorithm?}
\answer{It does not stop.  From $\bar z$ it walks down each edge to the
parameter it carries -- $\bar\theta_j=\bar z\,x_j$, the delta rule -- but
also to the \emph{input} $x_j$ of that edge: $\bar x_j=\bar z\,\theta_j$.  Since
$x_j$ is itself the output of a neuron, the sweep continues through its
activation, $\bar z_j=\bar x_j\,\sigma'(z_j)$, and then along its edges to
$\bm{w}_j$ and to the layer before.  Layer by layer, the adjoint of each
layer's output is multiplied by the transposed weight matrix and by the
derivative of the activation: that is \textbf{backpropagation}, and
Eq.~(5.41) is its one-layer special case.  The $\sigma'\le1/4$ factor is
multiplied in once per layer -- the reason deep sigmoid networks stop
learning, and the reason the activation function is chosen with care.}
\end{insight}

%% ====================================================================
%% Part 2: neural networks and deep learning (Sections 8.1-8.7)
%% ====================================================================

\begin{frame}{The artificial neuron (Section 8.1, book Figure 8.1)}
\begin{columns}
\begin{column}{0.42\textwidth}
\includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{8-neuron}
\end{column}
\begin{column}{0.56\textwidth}
\footnotesize
Eq.~(8.1): $n$ inputs, a weighted sum, a bias, a non-linearity,
$y=f\bigl(\sum_{i=1}^{n}w_ix_i+b\bigr)=f(z)$.
\begin{block}{Nothing new -- three models under one name}
\footnotesize
$f=$ identity: linear regression (Chapter 3).  $f=\sigma$: logistic
regression, exactly (Chapter 5).  $f=$ step: Rosenblatt's perceptron
(1958), the first machine-learning model.
\end{block}
\begin{block}{What changes now}
\footnotesize
In a \emph{network} the inputs of one neuron are the \emph{outputs} of
the neurons in the layer before.  \textbf{Fully connected}: every neuron
sees every output of the previous layer.  \textbf{Feed-forward}: information
flows one way, no cycles.  The features fed to the final sigmoid are then
\emph{learned}, not given -- that is the whole point, and the whole price:
no convexity, no closed form, only the chain rule.
\end{block}
\end{column}
\end{columns}
\end{frame}

\begin{frame}{Why one layer is not enough: XOR (Section 8.2, book Figure 8.2)}
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.78\textwidth]{xor_problem}\par
\vspace{-2pt}
\raggedright\footnotesize
\begin{columns}[T]
\begin{column}{0.5\textwidth}
Table 8.1: AND, OR and XOR of two binary inputs.  Least squares on XOR
returns $\bm{\theta}=(0.5,0,0)$ -- the best \emph{linear} function is the
constant $1/2$; logistic regression scores $0.50$, chance.  Not a training
failure: \textbf{the model class contains no solution}, because one neuron
computes one straight line and no line puts both circles on one side.  This
stalled the field for two decades (Minsky and Papert, 1969).
\end{column}
\begin{column}{0.46\textwidth}
\begin{block}{The remedy}
Two hidden neurons form two intermediate features -- ``$x_1$ or $x_2$'' and
``not both'' -- and the output neuron combines \emph{them} linearly:
accuracy $1.00$ on all three gates with the class of Section 8.13.  Seen
from Chapter 3: a basis expansion whose basis functions are \emph{fitted},
not chosen.
\end{block}
\end{column}
\end{columns}
\end{frame}

\begin{frame}{What a hidden layer supplies: the features it learned (\texttt{week40.ipynb})}
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.9\textwidth]{week40_xor_hidden}\par
\vspace{-2pt}
\raggedright\footnotesize
The 2--2--1 network we derive in Part 3 (sigmoid hidden nodes, sigmoid
output, squared error, plain gradient descent, $\gamma=2$, $20\,000$
iterations, seed 2026), trained on the four XOR points.  Each hidden node is
one neuron and draws one line: node 1 fires only near $(0,0)$ (``neither''),
node 2 only near $(1,1)$ (``both'').  In the plane of $(a_1^{1},a_2^{1})$ the
four points \emph{are} linearly separable, and the output neuron is the line
``not neither and not both'': outputs $0.01,0.99,0.99,0.01$.  A single neuron
trained the same way returns $0.5$ four times.  \textbf{Non-linearity is
essential}: with linear hidden units
$\bm{W}^{2}(\bm{W}^{1}\bm{x}+\bm{b}^{1})+\bm{b}^{2}$ is again affine in
$\bm{x}$ -- no depth of linear layers escapes linearity.
\end{frame}

\begin{frame}{Multilayer perceptrons and other architectures (Section 8.3, Figure 8.5)}
\begin{columns}[T]
\begin{column}{0.5\textwidth}
\includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{8-mlp}
\end{column}
\begin{column}{0.48\textwidth}
\footnotesize
A fully connected feed-forward network with an input layer, one or more
hidden layers and an output layer, non-linear activations throughout: the
\textbf{multilayer perceptron} (MLP) -- the architecture we analyse, and the
one for which backpropagation is simplest.  Here $L=3$: $M_0=3$ inputs, two
hidden layers of four, two outputs.
\begin{block}{Other architectures (later chapters)}
\footnotesize
\textbf{Convolutional}: a small kernel slid across the input, weights shared
across positions -- translation invariance, far fewer parameters; images.
\textbf{Recurrent}: the output of a layer fed back at the next time step --
a memory; sequences.  \textbf{Autoencoders}: a bottleneck between encoder
and decoder, trained to reproduce the input -- non-linear PCA.
\end{block}
\end{column}
\end{columns}
\end{frame}

\begin{frame}{Notation and the feed-forward pass (Section 8.5, book Figure 8.6)}
\begin{columns}[T]
\begin{column}{0.4\textwidth}
\includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{8-node}
\end{column}
\begin{column}{0.58\textwidth}
\footnotesize
Layers $l=0,1,\dots,L$ with $M_l$ nodes; $a_i^{0}=x_i$, $\tilde{\bm{y}}=\bm{a}^{L}$.
Node $j$ of layer $l$, Eqs.~(8.18)--(8.19):
\[
  z_j^{l}=\sum_{i=1}^{M_{l-1}}w_{ij}^{l}a_i^{l-1}+b_j^{l},\qquad a_j^{l}=f\bigl(z_j^{l}\bigr).
\]
$w_{ij}^{l}$: superscript = the layer it \emph{arrives} at, first index = the
node it \emph{leaves}, second = the node it arrives at; the forward sum runs
over the first index, the backward sum (next week) over the second.  Almost
every difficulty with backpropagation is a difficulty with indices.
\begin{block}{The feed-forward pass, Eqs.~(8.20)--(8.21)}
\scriptsize
$\bm{z}^{l}=(\bm{W}^{l})^{T}\bm{a}^{l-1}+\bm{b}^{l}$, $\bm{a}^{l}=f(\bm{z}^{l})$
for $l=1,\dots,L$; with $n$ samples as rows,
$\bm{Z}^{l}=\bm{A}^{l-1}\bm{W}^{l}+\bm{b}^{l}$ -- the form our code uses
(\texttt{X @ W1 + b1}), $\bm{W}^{l}$ untransposed; mixing the two is the
most frequent shape error.  Cost $\mathcal{O}(\sum_lM_{l-1}M_l)$ = the
number of weights: level-3 BLAS interleaved with element-wise $f$.
\end{block}
\end{column}
\end{columns}
\end{frame}

\begin{insight}[count the parameters, and the functions]
\question{A network for the $28\times28$ digit images has $784$ inputs, two
hidden layers of $100$ nodes and $10$ outputs.  How many parameters does it
have?  If the hidden activations were \emph{linear}, how many numbers would
it take to write down every function it can compute -- and what does that
say about the $100$ hidden nodes?}
\answer{Weights and biases: $784\cdot100+100+100\cdot100+100+100\cdot10+10=89\,610$.
With linear hidden layers the whole network is one affine map
$\bm{x}\mapsto\bm{A}\bm{x}+\bm{c}$ with $\bm{A}=\bm{W}^{1}\bm{W}^{2}\bm{W}^{3}$ of size
$784\times10$: at most $7\,850$ numbers matter, and since
$\mathrm{rank}\,\bm{A}\le100$ is no restriction for a $784\times10$ matrix,
it is exactly the multinomial logistic regression of Section 5.6 with an
$89\,610$-parameter way of writing $7\,850$ numbers.  The hidden nodes buy
nothing.  Everything the extra $81\,760$ parameters can do, they do through
the non-linearity of $f$ -- which is why the next frames are about which
$f$, and why the theorem after them requires $f$ not to be a polynomial.}
\end{insight}

\begin{frame}{The universal approximation theorem (Section 8.4)}
\small
\begin{columns}[T]
\begin{column}{0.5\textwidth}
\begin{block}{Cybenko (1989), Eq.~(8.3)}
\footnotesize
$\sigma$ continuous and sigmoidal ($\to1$ at $+\infty$, $\to0$ at $-\infty$),
$F$ continuous on $[0,1]^{d}$.  For every $\epsilon>0$ there is a
\emph{one-hidden-layer} network with
\[
  \bigl|F(\bm{x})-f(\bm{x};\bm{\Theta})\bigr|<\epsilon\quad\text{for all }\bm{x}\in[0,1]^{d}.
\]
Same shape of argument as Stone--Weierstrass for polynomials and the
convergence of Fourier series: a family of simple functions is dense.  To be
regarded with the same mixture of reassurance and scepticism.
\end{block}
\begin{block}{Hornik (1991), Eq.~(8.5)}
\footnotesize
Activation only non-constant and bounded, and the statement in mean square,
$\mathbb{E}|F-f|^{2}<\epsilon$ over the input density -- the expected
squared error of Section 2.10, not a worst case over a cube the data never
visit.  Universality is a property of the \emph{architecture}, not of
sigmoids.
\end{block}
\end{column}
\begin{column}{0.48\textwidth}
\begin{block}{Leshno, Lin, Pinkus, Schocken (1993): the sharp condition}
\footnotesize
One hidden layer is a universal approximator \textbf{if and only if the
activation is not a polynomial} on any interval.  So ReLU qualifies although
unbounded; every function of Section 8.6 qualifies; the \emph{one} that
fails is the linear activation -- Pause and think 2, recovered from the
general theory.
\end{block}
\begin{block}{A constructive version, Theorem 8.2, Eq.~(8.7)}
\footnotesize
In one dimension, $F$ $L$-Lipschitz on $[a,b]$: $N$ rectified units give
$\sup|F-g|\le L(b-a)/2N$ -- the network \emph{is} the piecewise-linear
interpolant at $N+1$ knots, and only the $N+1$ output weights depend on
$F$.  The classical proofs give no such relation between $\epsilon$ and the
width.
\end{block}
\end{column}
\end{columns}
\end{frame}

\begin{frame}{What the theorem does not say (Section 8.4)}
\footnotesize
\begin{enumerate}
  \item \textbf{Hidden layer only.}  The output nodes are linear; a sigmoid
        output could never represent the value $7$.  The output activation
        is dictated by the range of the target, the loss by its
        distribution (Section 3.6) -- never chosen independently.
  \item \textbf{Existence, not construction.}  The classical proofs relate
        $\epsilon$ to nothing -- not to the width (which may grow
        exponentially with $d$) and not to the size of the weights -- and
        say nothing about whether gradient descent on a non-convex cost will
        \emph{find} the weights.  \emph{Representability is not
        learnability}; the distance between the two is where the practical
        difficulty of deep learning lives (Section 8.4.4).
  \item \textbf{Universality is not remarkable.}  Polynomials, Fourier
        series, splines and radial basis functions are universal too.  What
        distinguishes networks is how \emph{economically} they approximate
        in high dimensions, and that \emph{depth} beats width: Theorem 8.6
        exhibits a function that a deep network represents with a number of
        units polynomial in the depth and a shallow one needs exponentially
        many for.  The theorem that names the field is about one hidden
        layer -- precisely the case it does not cover.
\end{enumerate}
\begin{block}{Hold it next to Chapter 3}
\footnotesize
Polynomials are universal, and the degree-14 fit of Figure 3.7 failed
catastrophically -- not because $f$ was unrepresentable but because the
coefficients could not be \emph{estimated} from a hundred noisy points.
Universality is a statement about the model class; generalisation is about
the class, the data and the estimator together, Eq.~(2.52).  A network large
enough to approximate anything is large enough to overfit anything.
\end{block}
\end{frame}

\begin{frame}{Activation functions: three families -- only the derivative matters (Section 8.6)}
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.6\textwidth]{activation_families}\par
\vspace{-3pt}
\raggedright\footnotesize
\textbf{Saturating}, Eq.~(8.22): $\sigma(z)$ with $\sigma'=\sigma(1-\sigma)\le1/4$;
$\tanh(z)=2\sigma(2z)-1$, centred, $\tanh'\le1$ -- both die in the tails.
\textbf{Rectified}, Eqs.~(8.23)--(8.25): $\mathrm{ReLU}(z)=\max(0,z)$, derivative
exactly $1$ or $0$ -- cheap, non-saturating, sparse; its failure is the
\emph{dying} unit (input negative for every sample $\Rightarrow$ zero gradient
forever); leaky ReLU ($\alpha z$ for $z<0$), ELU, SELU repair the negative
branch.  \textbf{Smooth gated}, Eqs.~(8.26)--(8.28): $\mathrm{GELU}(z)=z\Phi(z)$,
Swish $z\sigma(z)$, Mish -- non-monotone, derivative slightly above $1$,
twice differentiable (needed when the \emph{output} is differentiated, as
in physics-informed networks).  It is $f'$, not $f$, that enters the backward
recursion: it decides whether a gradient survives a layer.
\end{frame}

\begin{frame}{Which activation? Measure before believing the folklore (Table 8.2)}
\small
\begin{columns}[T]
\begin{column}{0.5\textwidth}
\includegraphics[width=0.9\textwidth]{activation_comparison}\\
\footnotesize Book Figure 8.7: a $64$--$30$--$30$--$30$--$10$ network on the
digits data, $60$ epochs, mean $\pm$ s.d.\ over five seeds.
\end{column}
\begin{column}{0.48\textwidth}
\begin{block}{What the table says}
\scriptsize
Sigmoid $0.890\pm0.010$, far behind, training loss two orders of magnitude
higher: with three hidden layers the $1/4$ per layer already bites.  The
seven non-saturating activations span $0.966$--$0.972$ -- \emph{less than
the spread between seeds} ($0.004$--$0.008$); $\tanh$ is nominally the best.
With $360$ test samples the standard error of an accuracy near $0.97$ is
$0.009$ (Section 2.5): a difference of $0.006$ was never resolvable.  A paper
declaring a winner from one run reports seed noise.
\end{block}
\begin{block}{Working rule}
\scriptsize
ReLU first; leaky ReLU or ELU if units die; GELU/Swish in transformers, very
deep networks, and wherever the output must be differentiated; $\tanh$ when
a bounded activation is wanted; the logistic function in a hidden layer only
to reproduce an old result.  \textbf{The output activation is not a free
choice}: identity for regression, sigmoid for two classes, softmax for $K$.
\end{block}
\end{column}
\end{columns}
\end{frame}

\begin{insight}[the derivative is what matters]
\question{Ten sigmoid layers in a chain, every pre-activation near zero so
that each $\sigma'$ is at its maximum.  By what factor is the gradient that
reaches the first layer at most attenuated?  And with ReLU?  With a
pre-activation of $z=5$ in every sigmoid layer instead?}
\answer{Each layer multiplies the error by $\sigma'(z^{l})\le1/4$ (and by a
weight), so at best $(1/4)^{10}\approx10^{-6}$: the first layer receives a
millionth of the signal and, at any sensible learning rate, does not move --
the \emph{vanishing gradient} of Section 8.9, and the reason the sigmoid
sits alone at the bottom of Table 8.2.  ReLU has derivative exactly one on
the active side: the factor is one for every active unit and the signal
arrives undiminished (the weights still scale it, which is the subject of
initialisation, Section 8.10).  At $z=5$, $\sigma'(5)=0.0066$ and ten layers
give $10^{-22}$: a saturated sigmoid network cannot learn at all, whatever the
depth of the error at the output.  We will see the mechanism with a single
hidden neuron in twenty minutes.}
\end{insight}

\begin{frame}{The cost function and the optimisation problem (Section 8.7)}
\small
Collect every weight and bias into $\bm{\Theta}=\{\bm{W}^{l},\bm{b}^{l}\}_{l=1}^{L}$,
Eqs.~(8.31)--(8.32):
\[
  \hat{\bm{\Theta}}=\operatorname*{arg\,min}_{\bm{\Theta}}\;
  \frac1n\sum_{i=1}^{n}L\bigl(y_i,\tilde y_i(\bm{\Theta})\bigr)+\lambda\Omega(\bm{\Theta}),
  \qquad
  C(\bm{\Theta})=\frac{1}{2n}\sum_{i=1}^{n}\bigl(a_i^{L}-y_i\bigr)^{2}
  \ \text{(regression)},
\]
the cross entropy (5.13)/(5.28) for classification -- the loss is still
decided by the distribution of the target, Section 3.6.
\begin{columns}[T]
\begin{column}{0.5\textwidth}
\begin{block}{Three things we have never met before}
\footnotesize
\textbf{Not convex}: linear maps composed with non-linearities -- local
minima, saddles, and the answer depends on the initialisation; Section 4.1
no longer protects us.  \textbf{Highly symmetric}: permute the hidden units
and nothing changes, so every minimum has a large family of copies.
\textbf{Very many parameters}: the Hessian of Section 4.2 is out of the
question -- first-order methods only, Sections 4.7--4.11.
\end{block}
\end{column}
\begin{column}{0.48\textwidth}
\begin{block}{What we are left with, and why it is enough}
\footnotesize
Gradient descent and the chain rule.  That such networks train at all is an
empirical fact, not a theorem (strict local minima are rare in high
dimensions relative to saddles; the symmetric copies make \emph{some} good
solution easy to reach).  The one thing we can make exact is the
\emph{gradient}: for the price of about two forward passes, whatever the
number of parameters -- reverse-mode AD, Section 4.14, written out by hand
for a layered network.  That is Part 3.
\end{block}
\end{column}
\end{columns}
\end{frame}

%% ====================================================================
%% Part 3: backpropagation from the smallest networks (Sections 8.8.1-8.8.2)
%% ====================================================================

\begin{frame}{The smallest network: no hidden layer (Section 8.8.1, Eqs.~8.33--8.37)}
\small
One node per layer, so only the layer superscript survives: $w^{1},b^{1},z^{1},a^{1}$
are numbers.  Sigmoid activation, half-squared error for one pair $(x,y)$:
\[
  x\;\longrightarrow\;z^{1}=w^{1}x+b^{1}\;\longrightarrow\;a^{1}=\sigma(z^{1})
  \;\longrightarrow\;C=\tfrac12(a^{1}-y)^{2}.
\]
The neuron of Figure 5.4 with $p=1$ and the loss swapped.  $C$ depends on
$w^{1}$ only along $w^{1}\to z^{1}\to a^{1}\to C$, so the chain rule (1.51):
\[
  \frac{\partial C}{\partial w^{1}}
  =\frac{\partial C}{\partial a^{1}}\frac{\partial a^{1}}{\partial z^{1}}\frac{\partial z^{1}}{\partial w^{1}}
  =(a^{1}-y)\,\sigma'(z^{1})\,x,\qquad
  \frac{\partial C}{\partial b^{1}}=(a^{1}-y)\,\sigma'(z^{1})
\]
(the same path, with $\partial z^{1}/\partial b^{1}=1$ as its last factor).
\begin{columns}[T]
\begin{column}{0.5\textwidth}
\begin{block}{Name the shared part: the error, Eq.~(8.35)}
\footnotesize
\[
  \delta^{1}=(a^{1}-y)\,\sigma'(z^{1})=\frac{\partial C}{\partial z^{1}},
  \quad
  \frac{\partial C}{\partial w^{1}}=\delta^{1}x,\ \
  \frac{\partial C}{\partial b^{1}}=\delta^{1},
\]
and one step of gradient descent, Eq.~(8.37):
$w^{1}\leftarrow w^{1}-\gamma\delta^{1}x$, $b^{1}\leftarrow b^{1}-\gamma\delta^{1}$
-- error times the signal that entered the parameter.
\end{block}
\end{column}
\begin{column}{0.48\textwidth}
\begin{block}{The delta rule once more -- with one difference}
\scriptsize
For the cross entropy $\partial C/\partial z$ was the bare residual
$\hat p-y$ (5.36): the softplus derivative cancelled $\sigma'$.  With the
squared error nothing cancels and the residual is multiplied by
$\sigma'(z^{1})\le1/4$, which dies once $|z^{1}|$ is a few units.  \emph{A
saturated neuron learns slowly however wrong it is}: the factor $f'$ that
will sit in front of every error, and the seed of Section 8.9.
\end{block}
\end{column}
\end{columns}
\end{frame}

\begin{frame}{The same neuron read backwards: $\delta$ is the adjoint of $z$ (Eqs.~8.38--8.39)}
\begin{columns}[T]
\begin{column}{0.5\textwidth}
\includegraphics[width=\textwidth,trim=0 138 100 0,clip]{8-scalargraph}
\footnotesize Book Figure 8.10, top panel: black the forward pass, red dashed
the reverse sweep.
\end{column}
\begin{column}{0.48\textwidth}
\footnotesize
Parameters as inputs, $v_{-1}=b^{1}$, $v_0=w^{1}$; data as constants.  The
trace, Eq.~(8.38):
\begin{align*}
  v_1&=v_0x, & v_2&=v_1+v_{-1}=z^{1},\\
  v_3&=\sigma(v_2)=a^{1}, & v_4&=\tfrac12(v_3-y)^{2}=C .
\end{align*}
Reverse sweep, Eq.~(4.87), from $\bar v_4=1$, Eq.~(8.39):
\begin{align*}
  \bar v_3&=v_3-y=a^{1}-y, & \bar v_2&=\bar v_3\,\sigma'(v_2)=\delta^{1},\\
  \bar v_0&=\bar v_2\,x=\delta^{1}x, & \bar v_{-1}&=\bar v_2=\delta^{1}.
\end{align*}
\begin{block}{Read it}
\scriptsize
The error $\delta^{1}$ \emph{is} $\bar z^{1}$, exactly as $\bar z=\hat p-y$
was in Figure 5.5, and Eq.~(8.36) is what the sweep delivers at the two
inputs.  One sweep, both gradients; forward mode (4.81) would need one per
parameter.  Backpropagation is this sweep continued through as many layers
as the network has.
\end{block}
\end{column}
\end{columns}
\end{frame}

\begin{frame}{One hidden layer: the recursion appears (Eqs.~8.40--8.43)}
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.56\textwidth,trim=0 0 0 128,clip]{8-scalargraph}\par
\vspace{-6pt}
\raggedright
\begin{columns}[T]
\begin{column}{0.4\textwidth}
\scriptsize
Book Figure 8.10, bottom panel.  Eq.~(8.40):
\begin{align*}
  z^{1}&=w^{1}x+b^{1}, & a^{1}&=\sigma(z^{1}),\\
  z^{2}&=w^{2}a^{1}+b^{2}, & a^{2}&=\sigma(z^{2}),\\
  C&=\tfrac12(a^{2}-y)^{2}.
\end{align*}
The forward pass computes \emph{and stores} $(z^{1},a^{1},z^{2},a^{2})$:
every one is needed on the way back.\\[3pt]
\textbf{Output layer} = the previous example with $a^{1}$ in place of $x$,
Eq.~(8.41):
\[
  \delta^{2}=(a^{2}-y)\sigma'(z^{2}),\ \
  \frac{\partial C}{\partial w^{2}}=\delta^{2}a^{1},\ \
  \frac{\partial C}{\partial b^{2}}=\delta^{2}.
\]
\end{column}
\begin{column}{0.58\textwidth}
\scriptsize
\textbf{Hidden weight}: the longer path $w^{1}\to z^{1}\to a^{1}\to z^{2}\to a^{2}\to C$,
Eq.~(8.42):
\[
  \frac{\partial C}{\partial w^{1}}
  =\underbrace{(a^{2}-y)\sigma'(z^{2})}_{\delta^{2}}\,w^{2}\,\sigma'(z^{1})\,x .
\]
The first two factors are $\delta^{2}$, \emph{already computed}.  With
$\delta^{1}=\partial C/\partial z^{1}$ as before, Eq.~(8.43):
\[
  \boxed{\;\delta^{1}=\delta^{2}\,w^{2}\,\sigma'(z^{1})\;}\quad
  \frac{\partial C}{\partial w^{1}}=\delta^{1}x,\ \
  \frac{\partial C}{\partial b^{1}}=\delta^{1}.
\]
\end{column}
\end{columns}
\end{frame}

\begin{frame}{The pattern -- and the reverse sweep that did not stop}
\small
\begin{columns}[T]
\begin{column}{0.5\textwidth}
\begin{block}{Forwards and backwards}
\footnotesize
Forwards, every layer computes $z^{l}=w^{l}a^{l-1}+b^{l}$ and
$a^{l}=\sigma(z^{l})$.  Backwards, every layer computes its error from the
error downstream, times the weight that connects them, times its own
$\sigma'(z^{l})$; the gradient of a weight is that error times the activation
that entered the weight, the gradient of a bias is the error itself.
\emph{Nothing is recomputed}: the long product of Eq.~(8.42) is never formed,
its first half is $\delta^{2}$ and was already evaluated for the output
layer.  With ten hidden neurons in a chain each error costs one
multiplication, each gradient one more.
\end{block}
\end{column}
\begin{column}{0.48\textwidth}
\begin{block}{In the language of Section 4.14}
\footnotesize
Eq.~(8.43) is the reverse sweep of Eq.~(8.39) that did not stop at the
pre-activation.  In Section 5.8.2 the sweep reached $\bar z=\hat p-y$ and
stepped onto the edges carrying the parameters, because the inputs of the
neuron were constants.  Here the input $a^{1}$ of the output neuron is the
output of a node, so the sweep continues: $\bar a^{1}=\delta^{2}w^{2}$ by the
local derivative $\partial z^{2}/\partial a^{1}=w^{2}$, then
$\bar z^{1}=\bar a^{1}\sigma'(z^{1})=\delta^{1}$, and only then the edges
$w^{1}$, $b^{1}$.  Pause and think 1, answered -- and the whole of
backpropagation, for one node per layer.
\end{block}
\end{column}
\end{columns}
\end{frame}

\begin{frame}[fragile]{Numbers, and the program that is Eqs.~(8.36)--(8.43) transcribed}
\begin{columns}[T]
\begin{column}{0.56\textwidth}
\begin{lstlisting}
def grad_one(w1, b1, x, y):             # no hidden layer
    z1 = w1*x + b1; a1 = sigma(z1)      # forward, store z1, a1
    delta1 = (a1 - y)*dsigma(z1)        # Eq. (8.35)
    return 0.5*(a1-y)**2, delta1*x, delta1, delta1

def grad_two(w1, b1, w2, b2, x, y):     # one hidden layer
    z1 = w1*x + b1; a1 = sigma(z1)      # forward pass
    z2 = w2*a1 + b2; a2 = sigma(z2)
    delta2 = (a2 - y)*dsigma(z2)        # output error, Eq. (8.41)
    delta1 = delta2*w2*dsigma(z1)       # Eq. (8.43)
    return (0.5*(a2-y)**2, delta1*x, delta1,
            delta2*a1, delta2, delta1, delta2)
\end{lstlisting}
\begin{lstlisting}
no hidden layer : C = 0.0362  delta1 = -0.0529
   dC/dw1 = -0.1058  dC/db1 = -0.0529
one hidden layer: C = 0.0979  delta2 = -0.1092  delta1 = -0.0215
   dC/dw1 = -0.0429  dC/db1 = -0.0215
   dC/dw2 = -0.0798  dC/db2 = -0.1092
\end{lstlisting}
\end{column}
\begin{column}{0.42\textwidth}
\footnotesize
At the point of Section 5.8.2, $w^{1}=0.25$, $b^{1}=0.5$, $x=2$, $y=1$:
$z^{1}=1$, $a^{1}=0.731$, $C=0.0362$, $\sigma'(z^{1})=0.197$,
$\delta^{1}=-0.0529$, gradient $(-0.106,-0.0529)$.
\begin{block}{Compare with the cross entropy}
\footnotesize
At the same point the cross entropy gave $(-0.538,-0.269)$ (Part 1).  The
ratio is exactly $\sigma'(z^{1})=0.197$: the factor that the squared error
does not cancel.
\end{block}
\begin{block}{Add the output neuron, $w^{2}=1$, $b^{2}=-0.5$}
\footnotesize
$z^{2}=0.231$, $a^{2}=0.557$, $C=0.0979$, $\delta^{2}=-0.109$, and by the
boxed recursion $\delta^{1}=\delta^{2}w^{2}\sigma'(z^{1})=-0.0215$.  Four
gradients, one forward pass, one backward pass, and every number reused.
\end{block}
\end{column}
\end{columns}
\end{frame}

\begin{frame}[fragile]{A first training program: the same equations with arrays (Section 8.8.1)}
\vspace{-3pt}
\begin{columns}[T]
\begin{column}{0.58\textwidth}
\begin{lstlisting}
def forwardpropagation(x):
    z_1 = x @ w_1 + b_1              # hidden pre-activation
    a_1 = sigmoid(z_1)
    z_2 = a_1 @ w_2 + b_2            # output pre-activation
    return a_1, z_2                  # linear output: a_2 = z_2

def backpropagation(x, y):
    a_1, a_2 = forwardpropagation(x)
    delta_2 = a_2 - y                # linear output: f' = 1
    delta_1 = (delta_2 @ w_2.T) * a_1 * (1 - a_1)   # Eq. (8.43)
    dW2 = a_1.T @ delta_2;  db2 = np.sum(delta_2, axis=0)
    dW1 = x.T @ delta_1;    db1 = np.sum(delta_1, axis=0)
    return dW2, db2, dW1, db1, 0.5*np.sum((a_2 - y)**2)

np.random.seed(0)                    # w_1, w_2 ~ randn(1, 1); biases 0.01
x = np.array([[4.0]]); y = 2*x + 1.0  # one sample, target 9
gamma = 0.1
for i in range(50):
    dW2, db2, dW1, db1, cost = backpropagation(x, y)
    w_2 -= gamma*dW2;  b_2 -= gamma*db2
    w_1 -= gamma*dW1;  b_1 -= gamma*db1
\end{lstlisting}
\end{column}
\begin{column}{0.4\textwidth}
\small
\begin{lstlisting}
iteration  0: cost = 36.895631
iteration 10: cost = 0.426990
iteration 20: cost = 0.004939
iteration 49: cost = 0.000000
w_1 = [1.7958]  b_1 = [0.0179];  w_2 = [4.6950]  b_2 = [4.3084]
\end{lstlisting}
\begin{block}{Two details that will survive unchanged}
\scriptsize
Samples are \emph{rows} (shape $(1,1)$ here), weights have shape (inputs,
outputs), the forward pass reads \texttt{x @ w\_1 + b\_1}, Eq.~(8.21).  The
output is \emph{linear}, so $\delta^{2}=a^{2}-y$ with no $f'$ -- Eq.~(8.69),
the natural pairing of a regression target with the squared error.
\texttt{delta\_2 @ w\_2.T} pulls the error back through the weight,
\texttt{x.T @ delta\_1} is the outer product of inputs and errors: for
scalars the multiplications of Eq.~(8.43), but Eqs.~(8.66) and (8.68) of
next week without a change to the code.  Not learning in any interesting
sense -- four parameters, one point -- but every line survives into the
2--2--1 network and into the class of Section 8.13.
\end{block}
\end{column}
\end{columns}
\end{frame}

\begin{frame}{The learning rate matters even here: a saturated hidden neuron (\texttt{week40.ipynb})}
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.92\textwidth]{week40_scalar_saturation}\par
\vspace{-2pt}
\raggedright\footnotesize
The program of the previous frame at $\gamma=0.1$ (blue) and $\gamma=1$
(red).  \textbf{(a)} At $\gamma=0.1$ the cost falls by nine orders of
magnitude in fifty iterations.  At $\gamma=1$ the first steps drive the
hidden weight to $w^{1}\approx4$, so $z^{1}\approx16$ \textbf{(b)}, and
$\sigma'(z^{1})\approx10^{-7}$ \textbf{(c)}: every gradient reaching the
first layer, $\delta^{1}x=\delta^{2}w^{2}\sigma'(z^{1})x$, is multiplied by
that factor, the hidden neuron stops learning, and the cost stalls at
$5.02$ for the remaining forty iterations however large the error at the
output.  This is the mechanism of Section 8.9 in a program of twenty lines.
Note the blue curve in (c) too: $\sigma'(z^{1})\approx7\times10^{-4}$
throughout -- see the next question.
\end{frame}

\begin{insight}[who did the learning?]
\question{In the successful run ($\gamma=0.1$) the hidden weight started at
$w^{1}=1.764$ (the first \texttt{randn} of seed 0) and ended at $1.796$,
while $\sigma'(z^{1})$ stayed near $7\times10^{-4}$.  Which parameters fitted
the target $9$, and why does the network still reach a cost of $10^{-8}$?
What would happen with a second training point at $x=-4$, $y=-7$?}
\answer{The output layer did all of it: with $a^{1}=\sigma(z^{1})\approx0.9993$
essentially constant, $a^{2}=w^{2}a^{1}+b^{2}$ is a linear function of two
free parameters $(w^{2},b^{2})$, and any single target can be hit with the
hidden neuron frozen -- $w^{2}$ went from $0.400$ to $4.695$ and $b^{2}$
from $0.01$ to $4.308$.  The gradient reaching $w^{1}$ was $\delta^{2}w^{2}
\sigma'(z^{1})x\sim10^{-3}\times$ the output error, so it hardly moved.  With
a second point at $x=-4$ the hidden neuron would have to output two
\emph{different} values, $a^{1}(4)\neq a^{1}(-4)$, and the frozen unit at
$0.9993$ for $x=4$ would give $\sigma(-7.0)\approx10^{-3}$ for $x=-4$: fine
by luck for this sign pattern, but in general the hidden layer must
\emph{learn}, and a saturated one cannot.  Two lessons for the class of
Section 8.13: initialise small (Section 8.10) so that $z^{1}$ starts in the
region where $\sigma'$ is not tiny, and watch $f'$, not only the cost.}
\end{insight}

\begin{frame}[fragile]{The same by automatic differentiation: we write the forward pass only}
\begin{columns}[T]
\begin{column}{0.64\textwidth}
\begin{lstlisting}
def C_one(p, x, y):                 # forward pass, single neuron
    return 0.5*(jax.nn.sigmoid(p[0]*x + p[1]) - y)**2
def C_two(p, x, y):                 # ... with the hidden neuron
    a1 = jax.nn.sigmoid(p[0]*x + p[1])
    return 0.5*(jax.nn.sigmoid(p[2]*a1 + p[3]) - y)**2

p1, p2 = jnp.array([0.25, 0.5]), jnp.array([0.25, 0.5, 1.0, -0.5])
print(jax.grad(C_one)(p1, 2.0, 1.0))   # [-0.1058 -0.0529]
print(jax.grad(C_two)(p2, 2.0, 1.0))   # [-0.0429 -0.0215 -0.0798 -0.1092]
\end{lstlisting}
\begin{lstlisting}
def cost(params, x, y):
    w_1, b_1, w_2, b_2 = params
    a_1 = jax.nn.sigmoid(x @ w_1 + b_1)     # the forward pass, nothing else
    return 0.5*jnp.sum((a_1 @ w_2 + b_2 - y)**2)
grad = jax.jit(jax.grad(cost))              # the backward pass, generated
for i in range(50):                         # same costs, same parameters
    g = grad(params, x, y)
    params = [p - gamma*gp for p, gp in zip(params, g)]
\end{lstlisting}
\end{column}
\begin{column}{0.35\textwidth}
\scriptsize
Every digit agrees with \texttt{grad\_one}/\texttt{grad\_two};
\texttt{jax.jvp} with the two unit seeds (notebook) returns the same two
numbers one at a time.
\begin{block}{What happened}
\scriptsize
Nothing in the listing mentions $\delta$, $\sigma'$ or the chain rule.
\texttt{jax.grad} traced the forward pass, recorded the tape
$(z^{1},a^{1},z^{2},a^{2})$ and swept it in reverse -- Eq.~(8.39)
continued through the hidden neuron.  The training program loses
\texttt{backpropagation} and prints the \emph{same} costs and parameters.
\end{block}
\begin{block}{Why we still write it by hand}
\scriptsize
The pattern of every framework from Chapter 10 on: we write the forward
pass, the library the backward one.  The hand-written version (Section
8.13) is the only way to know what the library does; the gradient check of
Eq.~(4.102) verifies that the two agree.  Project 2 asks for both.
\end{block}
\end{column}
\end{columns}
\end{frame}

\begin{frame}{Training the two networks on twenty points (book Figure 8.11)}
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.96\textwidth]{scalar_backprop}\par
\vspace{-3pt}
\raggedright\footnotesize
$n=20$ inputs in $[-2,2]$, $y_i=\sigma(2x_i-1)+\varepsilon_i$,
$\varepsilon_i\sim N(0,0.1^{2})$, cost averaged over the sample (the errors
$\delta^{l}$ of the observations simply add), $1000$ steps of plain gradient
descent with $\gamma=2$ from the parameters above.  \textbf{Left:} the cost
surface of the single neuron is \emph{not convex} -- the sigmoid sees to
that -- but has one basin around $(2,-1)$; the path from $(0.25,0.5)$ ends at
$(2.13,-0.78)$, $C=0.0053$.  \textbf{Middle:} the network with a hidden
neuron contains the single one as a special case and has four parameters,
yet reaches only $C=0.0066$ in the same number of steps.  \textbf{Right:}
why -- the gradient reaching $w^{1}$ is $\delta^{1}x$ in both, but in the
deeper network $\delta^{1}$ carries the extra factor $w^{2}\sigma'(z^{2})
\approx0.25$ of Eq.~(8.43), which shrinks the first-layer gradient by that
amount.  Every gradient was again compared with \texttt{jax.grad} at every
iteration: largest discrepancy $3\times10^{-17}$.
\end{frame}

\begin{frame}{Two inputs, two hidden nodes, one output (Section 8.8.2, book Figure 8.12)}
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.66\textwidth]{8-twotwo}\par
\vspace{-2pt}
\raggedright\footnotesize
The smallest network in which a weight needs two indices, Eqs.~(8.44)--(8.47):
$w_{ij}^{1}$ leaves input $i$ and arrives at hidden node $j$,
\[
  z_j^{1}=w_{1j}^{1}x_1+w_{2j}^{1}x_2+b_j^{1},\ a_j^{1}=\sigma(z_j^{1}),\ j=1,2;
  \quad
  z^{2}=w_1^{2}a_1^{1}+w_2^{2}a_2^{1}+b^{2},\ a^{2}=\sigma(z^{2}),\ C=\tfrac12(a^{2}-y)^{2},
\]
or $\bm{z}^{1}=(\bm{W}^{1})^{T}\bm{x}+\bm{b}^{1}$ with
$\bm{W}^{1}=\begin{bmatrix}w_{11}^{1}&w_{12}^{1}\\w_{21}^{1}&w_{22}^{1}\end{bmatrix}$,
Eq.~(8.45).  Nine parameters; the forward pass stores
$z_1^{1},z_2^{1},a_1^{1},a_2^{1},z^{2},a^{2}$.  This is the last time we
write the derivatives one by one.
\end{frame}

\begin{frame}{The nine derivatives, three at a time (Eqs.~8.48--8.52)}
\footnotesize
\textbf{Output layer} -- the scalar case with the input activation carrying
the index of its edge, Eq.~(8.48):
\[
  \delta^{2}=\frac{\partial C}{\partial z^{2}}=(a^{2}-y)\,\sigma'(z^{2}),\qquad
  \frac{\partial C}{\partial w_j^{2}}=\delta^{2}a_j^{1}\ (j=1,2),\qquad
  \frac{\partial C}{\partial b^{2}}=\delta^{2}.
\]
\textbf{Hidden layer} -- take $w_{11}^{1}$: it affects $z_1^{1}$ only, which
reaches $C$ only through $z^{2}$ along the edge $w_1^{2}$; the same
five-factor chain as Eq.~(8.42), Eq.~(8.49):
\[
  \frac{\partial C}{\partial w_{11}^{1}}
  =\underbrace{\frac{\partial C}{\partial a^{2}}\frac{\partial a^{2}}{\partial z^{2}}}_{\delta^{2}}
   \frac{\partial z^{2}}{\partial a_1^{1}}\frac{\partial a_1^{1}}{\partial z_1^{1}}
   \frac{\partial z_1^{1}}{\partial w_{11}^{1}}
  =\delta^{2}\,w_1^{2}\,\sigma'(z_1^{1})\,x_1 .
\]
$w_{21}^{1}$ (from $x_2$, same node) has the identical chain with $x_2$ last;
$b_1^{1}$ has the factor $1$.  So each hidden node has an error of its own,
Eqs.~(8.50)--(8.51), and collected, Eq.~(8.52):
\[
  \boxed{\;\delta_j^{1}=\delta^{2}\,w_j^{2}\,\sigma'(z_j^{1}),\qquad
  \frac{\partial C}{\partial w_{ij}^{1}}=\delta_j^{1}\,a_i^{0},\qquad
  \frac{\partial C}{\partial b_j^{1}}=\delta_j^{1},\qquad i,j\in\{1,2\}.\;}
\]
\begin{block}{The rule, now with indices (Eq.~8.53)}
\scriptsize
$\partial C/\partial w_{ij}^{l}=\delta_j^{l}a_i^{l-1}$, $\partial C/\partial b_j^{l}=\delta_j^{l}$:
\emph{the gradient of a weight is the error at the node it arrives at, times
the signal at the node it leaves}.  The error carries the receiving index,
the activation the sending index, and the gradient has the shape of the
weight matrix.
\end{block}
\end{frame}

\begin{frame}{Vector form, outer product -- and the fan-out the sweep did not need (8.54--8.56)}
\footnotesize
\begin{columns}[T]
\begin{column}{0.5\textwidth}
The two hidden errors as a vector, Eq.~(8.54),
\[
  \bm{\delta}^{1}=\begin{bmatrix}\delta_1^{1}\\ \delta_2^{1}\end{bmatrix}
  =\bigl(\bm{W}^{2}\delta^{2}\bigr)\circ\sigma'(\bm{z}^{1}),
  \qquad \bm{W}^{2}=\begin{bmatrix}w_1^{2}\\ w_2^{2}\end{bmatrix},
\]
and the four hidden weight gradients as an outer product, Eq.~(8.55),
\[
  \frac{\partial C}{\partial\bm{W}^{1}}=\bm{x}\,(\bm{\delta}^{1})^{T}
  =\begin{bmatrix}x_1\delta_1^{1}&x_1\delta_2^{1}\\ x_2\delta_1^{1}&x_2\delta_2^{1}\end{bmatrix},
\]
entry $(i,j)$ in the position of $w_{ij}^{1}$.  These are, for this network,
Eqs.~(8.66) and (8.68) of next week.  Update, Eq.~(8.56):
$w_{ij}^{l}\leftarrow w_{ij}^{l}-\gamma\delta_j^{l}a_i^{l-1}$,
$b_j^{l}\leftarrow b_j^{l}-\gamma\delta_j^{l}$, all nine at once.
\end{column}
\begin{column}{0.48\textwidth}
\begin{block}{Read as the reverse sweep of Section 4.14.4}
\scriptsize
\textbf{New:} the adjoint $\delta^{2}$ leaves the output node along
\emph{two} edges, $w_1^{2}$ and $w_2^{2}$ -- a fan-in seen from the output;
it costs nothing, each hidden node receives its own share.
\textbf{Still missing:} the fan-out.  Each hidden node feeds \emph{one} node
downstream, so its adjoint arrives in a single term and no sum is needed.
(The inputs $x_i$ do feed both hidden nodes, but they are constants of the
trace and we never need their adjoints.)
\end{block}
\begin{block}{The only thing the general case adds}
\scriptsize
Give the network a second output node: $z_j^{1}$ reaches $C$ along two
paths, the adjoints from the two outputs must be \emph{added} at each hidden
node, and Eq.~(8.52) acquires $\sum_k\delta_k^{2}w_{jk}^{2}$ -- the fan-out
rule, exactly as the two branches of Figure 5.5 were summed at $v_2$.  That
sum, Eq.~(8.64), is Section 8.8.3 -- next week.
\end{block}
\end{column}
\end{columns}
\end{frame}

\begin{frame}[fragile]{Code: the 2--2--1 network, five lines of backward pass, checked with AD}
\vspace{-2pt}
\begin{lstlisting}
def forward(X, W1, b1, W2, b2):
    A1 = sigmoid(X @ W1 + b1)                                 # hidden layer, samples in rows
    return A1, A1 @ W2 + b2                                   # linear output node
def backward(X, y, W1, b1, W2, b2):
    n = X.shape[0]
    A1, A2 = forward(X, W1, b1, W2, b2)
    delta2 = (A2 - y)/n                                       # output error, f' = 1, Eq. (8.48)
    delta1 = (delta2 @ W2.T) * A1*(1 - A1)                    # Eq. (8.54): (W2 delta2) o sigma'(z1)
    dW2 = A1.T @ delta2;  db2 = delta2.sum(axis=0)            # Eq. (8.53), l = 2
    dW1 = X.T @ delta1;   db1 = delta1.sum(axis=0)            # Eq. (8.55), summed over the samples
    return dW1, db1, dW2, db2, 0.5*np.sum((A2 - y)**2)/n
def cost(params, X, y):                                       # the same model, for jax.grad
    W1, b1, W2, b2 = params
    return 0.5*jnp.mean((jax.nn.sigmoid(X @ W1 + b1) @ W2 + b2 - y)**2)
g_hand = backward(X, y, W1, b1, W2, b2)[:4]                   # W1 (2, 2), W2 (2, 1) ~ N(0, 1), seed 41
g_jax  = jax.grad(cost)([jnp.array(p) for p in (W1, b1, W2, b2)], X, y)
print(max(float(jnp.max(jnp.abs(h - j))) for h, j in zip(g_hand, g_jax)))    # 1.78e-15
\end{lstlisting}
\vspace{-4pt}
\scriptsize
$\bm{X}$: $n=100$ rows, two columns; $\bm{W}^{1}$ is $2\times2$, $\bm{W}^{2}$
is $2\times1$.  Every line of \texttt{backward} is one of Eqs.~(8.48),
(8.54), (8.55) for all samples at once, \texttt{X.T @ delta1} summing the
outer products $\bm{x}_i(\bm{\delta}_i^{1})^{T}$.  Linear output, target
$y=x_1^{2}+3x_1x_2+x_2^{2}+5$, $\gamma=0.5$, $5000$ iterations: cost $22.76\to0.00113\to0.00081$
after $0/1000/5000$, $\bm{W}^{1}=\bigl[\begin{smallmatrix}2.524&1.048\\2.558&1.052\end{smallmatrix}\bigr]$,
$\bm{b}^{1}=(-4.12,-1.97)$, $\bm{W}^{2}=(5.08,2.98)$, $b^{2}=4.45$.  The
class of Section 8.13 has these five lines inside a loop over layers.
\end{frame}

\begin{frame}{The 2--2--1 network at work (\texttt{week40.ipynb})}
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.94\textwidth]{week40_twotwo}\par
\vspace{-2pt}
\raggedright\footnotesize
\textbf{(a)} The cost falls from $22.8$ to $10^{-3}$ in a thousand
iterations and then creeps to $C=0.00081$ -- a mean squared error of
$1.6\times10^{-3}$, rms error $0.04$ on targets between $5$ and $10$
\textbf{(b)}.  \textbf{(c)} The two learned features: both hidden nodes are
sigmoids of a linear function of $(x_1,x_2)$ -- ridges across the square,
$a_1^{1}$ steep along the diagonal, $a_2^{1}$ gentler -- and the linear
output node combines them, $a^{2}=5.08\,a_1^{1}+2.98\,a_2^{1}+4.45$, into a
surface that follows $x_1^{2}+3x_1x_2+x_2^{2}+5$ to $0.4\%$.  Two hidden
nodes, nine parameters, no polynomial features chosen by us.  What limits
the fit is the two-ridge shape; more hidden nodes would add more ridges --
the constructive theorem of Section 8.4.1 in two dimensions.
\end{frame}

\begin{insight}[a second output node]
\question{Give the 2--2--1 network a second output node, $z_k^{2}=\sum_jw_{jk}^{2}a_j^{1}+b_k^{2}$
for $k=1,2$, with the cost $\tfrac12\sum_k(a_k^{2}-y_k)^{2}$.  Which of the
nine-plus-three derivatives change their form, and what is the new
expression for $\delta_1^{1}$?  How does this read in the language of the
reverse sweep, and what is the vector form?}
\answer{The output layer is unchanged in form: $\delta_k^{2}=(a_k^{2}-y_k)\sigma'(z_k^{2})$,
$\partial C/\partial w_{jk}^{2}=\delta_k^{2}a_j^{1}$, one error per output
node.  What changes is the hidden error.  $z_1^{1}$ now reaches $C$ along
\emph{two} paths, through $z_1^{2}$ via $w_{11}^{2}$ and through $z_2^{2}$
via $w_{12}^{2}$, and the chain rule adds them:
$\delta_1^{1}=\bigl(\delta_1^{2}w_{11}^{2}+\delta_2^{2}w_{12}^{2}\bigr)\sigma'(z_1^{1})
=\bigl(\sum_k\delta_k^{2}w_{1k}^{2}\bigr)\sigma'(z_1^{1})$.  In the sweep:
$a_1^{1}$ has two successors, so its adjoint is the \emph{sum} of the two
contributions -- the fan-out rule of Section 4.14.4 that the 2--2--1 network
never needed.  Vector form: $\bm{\delta}^{1}=(\bm{W}^{2}\bm{\delta}^{2})\circ\sigma'(\bm{z}^{1})$
with $\bm{W}^{2}$ now $2\times2$ -- Eq.~(8.54) with a matrix in place of a
column, which is Eq.~(8.66), the backpropagation equation, in full
generality.  The weight gradients keep the outer-product form, Eq.~(8.68).
That is all next week adds.}
\end{insight}

\begin{frame}{Next week: the general case, the four equations, your own code (8.8.3--8.13)}
\small
\begin{columns}[T]
\begin{column}{0.5\textwidth}
\begin{block}{The four equations, Eqs.~(8.65)--(8.68)}
\scriptsize
\begin{align*}
  \bm{\delta}^{L}&=f'(\bm{z}^{L})\circ\partial C/\partial\bm{a}^{L}
   &&\text{(start at the output)}\\
  \bm{\delta}^{l}&=\bigl(\bm{W}^{l+1}\bm{\delta}^{l+1}\bigr)\circ f'(\bm{z}^{l})
   &&\text{(carry it back)}\\
  \partial C/\partial b_j^{l}&=\delta_j^{l},\quad
  \partial C/\partial w_{ij}^{l}=\delta_j^{l}a_i^{l-1}
   &&\text{(the gradients)}
\end{align*}
The second is Eq.~(8.64): the same graph traversed backwards with the
transposed weights (book Figure 8.13); the sum over $k$ is the fan-out we
just met.  For the natural output/loss pairings the first collapses to
$\bm{\delta}^{L}=\bm{a}^{L}-\bm{y}$, Eq.~(8.69).
\end{block}
\begin{block}{Cost}
\scriptsize
One matrix--vector product per layer, like the forward pass: the whole
gradient for about two forward passes whatever the number of parameters --
Prop.~4.2 for a layered network.  Finite differences, Eq.~(4.102): one pass
per parameter; for $10^{6}$ weights a second against a fortnight.
\end{block}
\end{column}
\begin{column}{0.48\textwidth}
\begin{block}{The algorithm (Section 8.8.5) and the class (Section 8.13)}
\scriptsize
Forward pass storing $\bm{z}^{l},\bm{a}^{l}$; output error; backward pass
$l=L-1,\dots,1$; gradients; update with any optimiser of Chapter 4, on
minibatches.  Then a \texttt{NeuralNetwork} class: a loop over layers, any
activation, both output heads, and its gradient check at $10^{-6}$--$10^{-8}$.
\end{block}
\begin{block}{And what makes it work}
\scriptsize
Vanishing/exploding gradients (8.9), initialisation (8.10), classification
heads (8.11), regularisation and early stopping (8.12).  Project 2 asks for
exactly this network, on the regression problem of Project 1 and on a
classification problem.
\end{block}
\end{column}
\end{columns}
\end{frame}

%% ====================================================================
%% Summary
%% ====================================================================

\begin{frame}{Summary of week 40}
\footnotesize
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{The reminder:} logistic regression is one neuron; its
        learning rule is error times the input on the edge (5.36--5.37); read
        as a computational graph, one reverse sweep gives the whole gradient
        for $1.4$--$2$ evaluations of the cost whatever $p$, forward mode
        needs $p$ sweeps (5.40--5.42).  Training asks the reverse-mode
        question.
  \item \textbf{Networks:} a neuron whose inputs are outputs of other
        neurons; one layer draws one line and cannot do XOR, a hidden layer
        learns the features that make the problem linear; non-linear $f$ is
        essential, and \emph{not a polynomial} is the exact condition for
        universality (8.4) -- an existence statement that says nothing about
        width, weights or learnability.  Only $f'$ enters the backward pass:
        sigmoid $\le1/4$ per layer, ReLU $1$.  The cost (8.31) is non-convex,
        symmetric and huge: first-order methods, and an exact gradient.
  \item \textbf{Backpropagation from the smallest networks:} the error
        $\delta=\partial C/\partial z$ is the adjoint of the pre-activation
        (8.35, 8.39); a weight's gradient is $\delta$ times its input; one
        hidden layer gives the recursion $\delta^{1}=\delta^{2}w^{2}\sigma'(z^{1})$
        (8.43) -- the reverse sweep that does not stop; the 2--2--1 network
        fixes the indices, $\partial C/\partial w_{ij}^{l}=\delta_j^{l}a_i^{l-1}$
        (8.53), vector and outer-product form (8.54--8.55); \texttt{jax.grad}
        of the forward pass reproduces every digit; a saturated hidden
        neuron stops learning (the $\gamma=1$ run).
  \item \textbf{What is left:} the sum over receiving nodes when a node
        feeds several -- the fan-out rule -- and with it the four equations
        (8.65--8.68), the algorithm and the class.  Next Monday.
\end{itemize}
\begin{block}{Tomorrow and the deadline}
Tuesday is \emph{only} Project 1: your questions, then two cases on reading,
analysing and presenting your own results.  No weekly exercises.  \textbf{Project 1 is due Monday October 5.}
\end{block}
\end{frame}

